IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `sacat-dates`
IMPORT `sacat-applicant`

-- South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal Act 2013 (SA) -- cases for the applicant's seat
-- (sacat-applicant.l4), with a few on the date and deadline helpers it composes. Every expected value was worked by hand
-- from the deposited Act and Regulations (the provision is named on each group), never copied from the encoding's
-- output. Edges: the 21st day and the 22nd (r 6); the last day of the month and the day after (ss 70(2), 71(3)), with
-- months that have no corresponding day; lodging on the last day of a limitation period (s 38C(2)); an amount equal to
-- the Magistrates Court limit and one dollar over (s 89(3)); each limb of every "or" and the order in which provisions
-- displace one another.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases: the applicant`

d MEANS `the calendar date`

§§ `Dates: one calendar month (fork F1)`

#ASSERT `the last day of one month after` (d 15 3 2025) EQUALS d 15 4 2025
#ASSERT `the last day of one month after` (d 31 1 2025) EQUALS d 28 2 2025
#ASSERT `the last day of one month after` (d 31 1 2024) EQUALS d 29 2 2024
#ASSERT `the last day of one month after` (d 28 2 2025) EQUALS d 28 3 2025
#ASSERT `the last day of one month after` (d 15 12 2025) EQUALS d 15 1 2026
#ASSERT `the last day of one month after` (d 31 10 2025) EQUALS d 30 11 2025
#ASSERT `the date` 21 `days after` (d 3 3 2025) EQUALS d 24 3 2025

§§ `ss 32-34, r 5: review or original jurisdiction`

`review` MEANS `review jurisdiction: the Tribunal rehears the decision and must reach the correct or preferable decision, giving appropriate weight to the original decision (s 34(1), (3), (4))`
`original` MEANS `original jurisdiction: the Tribunal decides the matter itself, resolves the dispute or takes another course it considers appropriate (s 33)`
`Crown` MEANS `the Crown, a government agency or instrumentality of the Crown`

-- A licensing refusal by a government agency.
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `Crown` `none of these` EQUALS `review`
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `a person or body prescribed by the regulations` `none of these` EQUALS `review`
-- s 34(2)(a)-(c) all fail: a private person's decision of no prescribed kind.
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `someone else` `none of these` EQUALS `original`
-- r 5(2): a community housing provider decision is prescribed, even from a private provider.
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `someone else` `a community housing decision under the Community Housing Providers (National Law) Schedule 2 clause 2 or a transitioning co-operative's s 84 application` EQUALS `review`
-- r 5(1): a public landlord's tenancy decision, park and retirement village decisions are excluded "even if" Crown.
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `Crown` `part of a tenancy dispute under the Residential Tenancies Act 1995` EQUALS `original`
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `Crown` `part of a residential park dispute under the Residential Parks Act 2007` EQUALS `original`
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `Crown` `a retirement village decision under the Retirement Villages Act 1987 s 31(8) or in a dispute with a resident` EQUALS `original`
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` TRUE `Crown` `a retirement village decision under the Retirement Villages Act 2016 s 44(8) or in a dispute with a resident` EQUALS `original`
-- No decision to review: a claim (s 33(1)), whoever the respondent.
#ASSERT `Is my matter in the review or the original jurisdiction` FALSE `Crown` `none of these` EQUALS `original`

§§ `s 35(2), r 6: the decision-maker's reasons and documents`

#ASSERT `By when must the decision-maker give its reasons and documents` (d 3 3 2025) EQUALS d 24 3 2025
#ASSERT `By when must the decision-maker give its reasons and documents` (d 20 12 2025) EQUALS d 10 1 2026
-- notice received 3 March 2025; given on the 21st day (24 March), and the 22nd (25 March).
#ASSERT `Has the decision-maker given its reasons in time` (d 3 3 2025) TRUE (d 24 3 2025) (d 1 1 2026) EQUALS `given within the 21 days (s 35(2), r 6)`
#ASSERT `Has the decision-maker given its reasons in time` (d 3 3 2025) TRUE (d 25 3 2025) (d 1 1 2026) EQUALS `late: the 21 days have passed without them; the Tribunal can require them by written notice (s 35(2), (4)-(6))`
#ASSERT `Has the decision-maker given its reasons in time` (d 3 3 2025) TRUE (d 4 3 2025) (d 1 1 2026) EQUALS `given within the 21 days (s 35(2), r 6)`
-- not given: today the 21st day, then the 22nd.
#ASSERT `Has the decision-maker given its reasons in time` (d 3 3 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) (d 24 3 2025) EQUALS `not yet late: the decision-maker has until the 21st day after receiving notice (s 35(2), r 6)`
#ASSERT `Has the decision-maker given its reasons in time` (d 3 3 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) (d 25 3 2025) EQUALS `late: the 21 days have passed without them; the Tribunal can require them by written notice (s 35(2), (4)-(6))`
#ASSERT `s 35(2) -- reasons given on this day are in time` 21
#ASSERT (NOT (`s 35(2) -- reasons given on this day are in time` 22))

§§ `ss 36, 73: does the review or appeal stop the decision`

`s36` MEANS `my application to SACAT for review of a decision-maker's decision (s 36)`
`s73` MEANS `an internal review or appeal against a decision of SACAT (s 73)`

#ASSERT `Does the review or appeal stop the decision` `s36` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the proceedings run (s 36(1))`
#ASSERT `Does the review or appeal stop the decision` `s36` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the relevant Act says the decision does not operate while it is reviewed (s 36(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Does the review or appeal stop the decision` `s36` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `stayed or varied: an order under s 36(2) stays or varies the decision, on its conditions`
#ASSERT `Does the review or appeal stop the decision` `s73` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the internal review or appeal runs (s 73(1))`
#ASSERT `Does the review or appeal stop the decision` `s73` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `stayed: an order under s 73(2) stays the decision until the proceedings are finally decided, on its conditions`
-- s 73 has no relevant-Act exception: the flag does not count.
#ASSERT `Does the review or appeal stop the decision` `s73` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the decision keeps operating and can be implemented while the internal review or appeal runs (s 73(1))`

§§ `ss 37, 38: after the review`

`no again` MEANS `no: the decision as affirmed, varied or substituted by the Tribunal is not again open to review before the Tribunal; you may appeal under s 71 (s 37(5))`

#ASSERT `Can the decision be reviewed again` `the Tribunal affirmed the decision` EQUALS `no again`
#ASSERT `Can the decision be reviewed again` `the Tribunal varied the decision` EQUALS `no again`
#ASSERT `Can the decision be reviewed again` `the Tribunal set the decision aside and substituted its own` EQUALS `no again`
#ASSERT `Can the decision be reviewed again` `the Tribunal sent the matter back and the decision-maker has made a decision on reconsideration` EQUALS `yes: a decision made on reconsideration after the Tribunal sent the matter back is open to review by the Tribunal (s 37(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the decision be reviewed again` `on the Tribunal's invitation the decision-maker affirmed its decision` EQUALS `your review goes on: the decision-maker affirmed, so the Tribunal resumes the review of the same decision (s 38(2)(a), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can the decision be reviewed again` `on the Tribunal's invitation the decision-maker varied its decision or substituted a new one` EQUALS `your review goes on: unless you withdraw it, it is now a review of the varied or substituted decision (s 38(3))`

#ASSERT `From when does the review decision take effect` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `from when the reviewed decision took, or would have taken, effect (s 37(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `From when does the review decision take effect` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `from the time the relevant Act says (s 37(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `From when does the review decision take effect` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `from the time the Tribunal orders (s 37(3)(b))`
-- the relevant Act is read first ("unless the relevant Act states otherwise or the Tribunal orders otherwise").
#ASSERT `From when does the review decision take effect` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `from the time the relevant Act says (s 37(3)(b))`

§§ `Part 3A: transfer to the Magistrates Court`

-- lodged 10 June 2025; limitation period's last day 10 June 2025 (lodged on it), then 9 June 2025 (lodged a day late).
#ASSERT `Does my transferred claim count as made in time` (d 10 6 2025) TRUE (d 10 6 2025) EQUALS `yes: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal, which was within the limitation period (s 38C(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Does my transferred claim count as made in time` (d 10 6 2025) TRUE (d 9 6 2025) EQUALS `no: you lodged with the Tribunal after the limitation period expired, so s 38C(2) does not override it`
#ASSERT `Does my transferred claim count as made in time` (d 10 6 2025) TRUE (d 30 6 2027) EQUALS `yes: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal, which was within the limitation period (s 38C(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Does my transferred claim count as made in time` (d 10 6 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) EQUALS `no limitation period applies: the proceedings are taken to have started on the day you lodged with the Tribunal (s 38C(1))`

#ASSERT `Must I pay another fee for the transfer` FALSE EQUALS `no: the fee is the Tribunal's application fee, and no fee is payable for the transfer itself (s 38B(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Must I pay another fee for the transfer` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Magistrates Court has determined that additional fees are payable because the claims have substantially changed (s 38B(6))`

§§ `ss 29, 56, 60: appearing and the hearing`

`counsel` MEANS `counsel (a legal practitioner)`
`other rep` MEANS `another representative (not a lawyer)`

#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `myself` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: a party may appear personally (s 56(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `counsel` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: a party may appear by counsel (s 56(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `counsel` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: that person may not act as a representative (s 56(4))`
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `other rep` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a representative other than counsel needs the Tribunal's leave (s 56(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `other rep` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Tribunal has given leave, subject to the rules (s 56(1)(c))`
-- leave does not cure s 56(4): a struck-off former lawyer given leave still may not act.
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `other rep` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: that person may not act as a representative (s 56(4))`
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `counsel` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the relevant Act decides: s 56(1) applies subject to it`
-- appearing yourself: the barred flag is about a representative and does not count.
#ASSERT `Who can appear for me` `myself` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: a party may appear personally (s 56(1)(a))`

#ASSERT `Can a friend help me at the hearing` FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may be assisted by another person as a friend (s 56(2))`
#ASSERT `Can a friend help me at the hearing` TRUE EQUALS `no: the Tribunal has determined otherwise (s 56(2))`

#ASSERT `Can a member with a conflict hear my case` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 29 does not apply: the member has no conflicting interest`
#ASSERT `Can a member with a conflict hear my case` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the member must disclose the interest, and may take part because every party consents and the President has not directed withdrawal (s 29)`
#ASSERT `Can a member with a conflict hear my case` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the member must not take part, because the President has directed the member to withdraw (s 29(b)(i))`
#ASSERT `Can a member with a conflict hear my case` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the member must not take part, because a party (you or another) does not consent (s 29(b)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Can a member with a conflict hear my case` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the member must not take part, because a party (you or another) does not consent (s 29(b)(ii))`

#ASSERT `Will my hearing be in public` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: proceedings must be heard in public (s 60(1))`
#ASSERT `Will my hearing be in public` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Tribunal has directed that the hearing, or that part, be held in private (s 60(2)(e))`
#ASSERT `Will my hearing be in public` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the other Act decides: s 60(1) is subject to it`

§§ `ss 50, 51: conferences and mediation`

`conf` MEANS `a compulsory conference (s 50)`
`med` MEANS `a mediation (s 51)`

#ASSERT `Can what was said at the conference or mediation be used` `conf` FALSE EQUALS `no: evidence of anything said or done at a compulsory conference is inadmissible before the Tribunal (s 50(10))`
#ASSERT `Can what was said at the conference or mediation be used` `med` FALSE EQUALS `no: evidence of anything said or done at a mediation is inadmissible before the Tribunal (s 51(11))`
#ASSERT `Can what was said at the conference or mediation be used` `conf` TRUE EQUALS `yes: every party consents, so it may be used (ss 50(10), 51(11))`
#ASSERT `Can what was said at the conference or mediation be used` `med` TRUE EQUALS `yes: every party consents, so it may be used (ss 50(10), 51(11))`

#ASSERT `Can the conference member hear my case` `conf` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the member who presided at the conference is disqualified from hearing the matter (s 50(13))`
#ASSERT `Can the conference member hear my case` `conf` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: every party agrees to the member continuing (ss 50(13), 51(12))`
#ASSERT `Can the conference member hear my case` `med` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the member who mediated cannot take any further part in the proceedings (s 51(12))`
#ASSERT `Can the conference member hear my case` `med` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: every party agrees to the member continuing (ss 50(13), 51(12))`
#ASSERT `Can the conference member hear my case` `med` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 51(12) does not apply: the mediator was not a member of the Tribunal`
-- s 50(13) speaks of "the member of the Tribunal who presided": the mediator flag does not reach a conference.
#ASSERT `Can the conference member hear my case` `conf` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the member who presided at the conference is disqualified from hearing the matter (s 50(13))`

§§ `ss 46-49, 62(4): withdrawal, dismissal, starting again`

#ASSERT `Can I withdraw my application` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: you can withdraw only with the Tribunal's leave (s 47(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I withdraw my application` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Tribunal has given leave (s 47(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Can I withdraw my application` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the rules let you withdraw without leave (s 47(1), (2))`

`no bar` MEANS `this Act sets no bar: it does not stop a new proceeding after this kind of dismissal (the relevant Act, limitation periods and the general law may)`

#ASSERT `Can I start the proceeding again` `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, trivial, improper or an abuse of process (s 48)` EQUALS `only with the leave of a Presidential member: no new proceeding of the same kind on the same matter without it (s 48(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I start the proceeding again` `dismissed because I conducted it so as to disadvantage another party (s 49)` EQUALS `only with the leave of a Presidential member: no new proceeding of the same kind on the same matter without it (s 49(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I start the proceeding again` `I withdrew and it was dismissed (s 47(3))` EQUALS `no bar`
#ASSERT `Can I start the proceeding again` `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))` EQUALS `no bar`
#ASSERT `Can I start the proceeding again` `dismissed because I did not give security or an undertaking as ordered (s 62(4))` EQUALS `no bar`
#ASSERT `Can I start the proceeding again` `struck out because another tribunal or court is more appropriate (s 46)` EQUALS `no bar`

#ASSERT `What can the Tribunal do about conduct causing disadvantage` FALSE `me, the applicant` EQUALS `s 49 does not apply: the Tribunal does not believe a party is conducting the proceedings to another's unnecessary disadvantage`
#ASSERT `What can the Tribunal do about conduct causing disadvantage` TRUE `me, the applicant` EQUALS `your proceedings may be dismissed or struck out (s 49(2)(a)), and only a Presidential member can let you start again (s 49(4))`
#ASSERT `What can the Tribunal do about conduct causing disadvantage` TRUE `another party` EQUALS `the Tribunal may decide the proceedings in your favour and make appropriate orders, or strike the other party out (s 49(2)(b))`

#ASSERT `What happens if I do not give security` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `your proceedings may be dismissed with costs, or decided against you with costs (s 62(4))`
#ASSERT `What happens if I do not give security` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `you are not in default of a security order`
#ASSERT `What happens if I do not give security` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `you are not in default of a security order`

§§ `ss 57, 58, r 7: party costs`

`own` MEANS `each party bears its own costs (s 57(1))`
`should` MEANS `the Tribunal should order costs against the party the dismissal is directed against, unless it finds a good reason not to (s 57(3), r 7)`
`good reason` MEANS `each party bears its own costs: the Tribunal found a good reason not to order costs (s 57(1), (3))`

#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `it has not been dismissed or struck out` FALSE EQUALS `own`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` TRUE FALSE `it has not been dismissed or struck out` FALSE EQUALS `the relevant Act decides costs (s 57(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE TRUE `it has not been dismissed or struck out` FALSE EQUALS `the Tribunal has ordered costs (s 57(2)); they may include compensation for expenses or loss from the proceedings (s 58(1))`
-- r 7 prescribes s 47(4), s 48(2), s 49(2); not s 47(3) or other grounds.
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))` FALSE EQUALS `should`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48(2))` FALSE EQUALS `should`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed, or decided, for conduct causing disadvantage (s 49(2))` FALSE EQUALS `should`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed after withdrawal (s 47(3))` FALSE EQUALS `own`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed or struck out on another ground` FALSE EQUALS `own`
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48(2))` TRUE EQUALS `good reason`
-- the good-reason finding matters only for a prescribed dismissal.
#ASSERT `Who pays the legal costs` FALSE FALSE `dismissed after withdrawal (s 47(3))` TRUE EQUALS `own`

§§ `s 91, r 11: the Tribunal's costs of the proceedings`

`r11 yes` MEANS `yes: in the review jurisdiction, because the proceedings were dismissed or struck out in a prescribed circumstance (s 91(3)(b), r 11)`
`s91 no` MEANS `no: in the review jurisdiction the Tribunal cannot order you to pay the costs of the proceedings (s 91(3))`

#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `original jurisdiction` FALSE `it has not been dismissed or struck out` EQUALS `yes: in the original jurisdiction the Tribunal may order a party to pay all or any of the costs of the proceedings (s 91(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` FALSE `it has not been dismissed or struck out` EQUALS `s91 no`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` TRUE `it has not been dismissed or struck out` EQUALS `yes: in the review jurisdiction, because the party brought or conducted the proceedings frivolously or vexatiously (s 91(3)(a))`
-- r 11 includes s 47(3), unlike r 7.
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` FALSE `dismissed after withdrawal (s 47(3))` EQUALS `r11 yes`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` FALSE `dismissed for want of prosecution (s 47(4))` EQUALS `r11 yes`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` FALSE `dismissed as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse (s 48(2))` EQUALS `r11 yes`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` FALSE `dismissed, or decided, for conduct causing disadvantage (s 49(2))` EQUALS `r11 yes`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay the Tribunal's costs` `review jurisdiction` FALSE `dismissed or struck out on another ground` EQUALS `s91 no`

§§ `s 66: more time`

#ASSERT `Can the Tribunal give me more time` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the rules let the Tribunal extend the time limit, even after it has passed, on conditions it sets (s 66)`
#ASSERT `Can the Tribunal give me more time` FALSE EQUALS `not under s 66: this Act lets the Tribunal extend a time limit only where its rules provide for it`

§§ `s 70, r 4, s 38B(7): internal review`

#ASSERT `Can I apply for internal review` `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, with the leave of a legally qualified member of the Tribunal (s 70(1)(a), (1a)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for internal review` `a registrar or other staff member, in the review jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, with the leave of a Presidential member of the Tribunal (s 70(1)(b), (1a)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for internal review` `a registrar or other staff member, in the original jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, with leave: the decision is both in the original jurisdiction and by a registrar or staff member, so the Act names both a legally qualified member and a Presidential member (s 70(1a))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for internal review` `Tribunal members, in the review jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not under s 70: a decision of Tribunal members in the review jurisdiction is not open to internal review; it may be appealed (s 71)`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for internal review` `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: that interlocutory direction or order is not a "decision" for internal review or appeal (s 3(1), r 4)`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for internal review` `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: an order transferring the application to the Magistrates Court cannot be reviewed or appealed under Part 5 (s 38B(7))`

-- s 70(2): decision 15 March 2025, last day 15 April 2025.
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for internal review` (d 15 3 2025) EQUALS d 15 4 2025
#ASSERT `By when must I apply for internal review` (d 31 1 2025) EQUALS d 28 2 2025
#ASSERT `s 70(2) -- an application on this day is in time` (d 15 3 2025) (d 15 4 2025)
#ASSERT (NOT (`s 70(2) -- an application on this day is in time` (d 15 3 2025) (d 16 4 2025)))
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 15 3 2025) TRUE (d 15 4 2025) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `in time: within 1 month of the decision`
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 15 3 2025) TRUE (d 16 3 2025) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `in time: within 1 month of the decision`
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 15 3 2025) TRUE (d 16 4 2025) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 15 3 2025) TRUE (d 16 4 2025) (d 1 1 2026) TRUE EQUALS `late, but the time limit has been dispensed with`
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 15 3 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) (d 15 4 2025) FALSE EQUALS `not yet late: the month has not run out`
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 15 3 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) (d 16 4 2025) FALSE EQUALS `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`
-- 31 January 2025: February has no 31st, so the month ends on 28 February.
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 31 1 2025) TRUE (d 28 2 2025) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `in time: within 1 month of the decision`
#ASSERT `Is my internal review application in time` (d 31 1 2025) TRUE (d 1 3 2025) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`

§§ `s 71: appeals`

`CoA` MEANS `to the Court of Appeal, with the leave of the Supreme Court (s 71(1)(a), (2))`
`single` MEANS `to a single Judge of the Supreme Court, with the leave of the Supreme Court (s 71(1)(b), (2))`

#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a Presidential member alone` FALSE EQUALS `CoA`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `2 or 3 members including a Presidential member` FALSE EQUALS `CoA`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `members none of whom is a Presidential member` FALSE EQUALS `single`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a registrar or other staff member` FALSE EQUALS `single`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a Presidential member alone` TRUE EQUALS `the relevant Act decides: s 71 applies subject to its provisions on review and appeal`

`first` MEANS `yes: you cannot appeal until a s 70 internal review of the decision has been conducted (s 71(2a))`

#ASSERT `Must I seek internal review before appealing` `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `first`
#ASSERT `Must I seek internal review before appealing` `a registrar or other staff member, in the review jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `first`
#ASSERT `Must I seek internal review before appealing` `a registrar or other staff member, in the original jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `first`
#ASSERT `Must I seek internal review before appealing` `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: an internal review has already been conducted (s 71(2a))`
#ASSERT `Must I seek internal review before appealing` `Tribunal members, in the original jurisdiction` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the requirement is displaced for this decision (s 71(2b))`
#ASSERT `Must I seek internal review before appealing` `Tribunal members, in the review jurisdiction` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the decision was made by Tribunal members in the review jurisdiction, so s 71(2a) does not apply`

#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (d 31 1 2024) EQUALS d 29 2 2024
#ASSERT `By when must I appeal` (d 31 10 2025) EQUALS d 30 11 2025
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (d 31 1 2024) TRUE (d 29 2 2024) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `in time: within 1 month of the decision`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (d 31 1 2024) TRUE (d 1 3 2024) (d 1 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (d 31 1 2024) TRUE (d 1 3 2024) (d 1 1 2026) TRUE EQUALS `late, but the time limit has been dispensed with`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (d 15 12 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) (d 15 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `not yet late: the month has not run out`
#ASSERT `Is my appeal in time` (d 15 12 2025) FALSE (d 1 1 2000) (d 16 1 2026) FALSE EQUALS `late: more than 1 month after the decision; it can proceed only if the time limit is dispensed with, on being satisfied that this is just and reasonable`
#ASSERT `s 71(3) -- an appeal on this day is in time` (d 15 12 2025) (d 15 1 2026)
#ASSERT (NOT (`s 71(3) -- an appeal on this day is in time` (d 15 12 2025) (d 16 1 2026)))

§§ `ss 84, 85: correcting and reopening`

`fixable` MEANS `yes: you may apply, under the rules, for the Tribunal to correct it (s 84)`

#ASSERT `Can I have a mistake corrected` `a clerical mistake` EQUALS `fixable`
#ASSERT `Can I have a mistake corrected` `an error from an accidental slip or omission` EQUALS `fixable`
#ASSERT `Can I have a mistake corrected` `a material miscalculation of figures or a material mistake in describing a person, thing or matter` EQUALS `fixable`
#ASSERT `Can I have a mistake corrected` `a defect of form` EQUALS `fixable`
#ASSERT `Can I have a mistake corrected` `an error of fact, law or judgment in the decision itself` EQUALS `not under s 84: it reaches only clerical mistakes, slips, material miscalculations or misdescriptions, and defects of form; seek internal review or appeal`

`absent no` MEANS `no: s 85 is only for a person who neither appeared nor was represented at a hearing at which, or before which, the decision was made`

#ASSERT `Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Tribunal is to review the decision and may revoke or vary it (s 85(5))`
#ASSERT `Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `you may apply, but the Tribunal is not satisfied you had a reasonable excuse, so s 85(5) does not require it to review the decision`
#ASSERT `Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `absent no`
#ASSERT `Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `absent no`
#ASSERT `Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a mediation is not a "relevant hearing" for s 85`
#ASSERT `Can the decision made in my absence be reviewed` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the application must be made within the time limits in the rules (s 85(3))`

§§ `ss 38I(1), 89, r 9: enforcing a money order`

-- limit given as $100,000 for the test (an input; the Magistrates Court Act 1991 is not deposited).
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` TRUE FALSE 100000 100000 FALSE EQUALS `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 89(1), (3)(a), r 9)`
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` TRUE FALSE 100001 100000 FALSE EQUALS `you can recover it as a debt in the District Court (s 89(1), (3)(b), r 9)`
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` TRUE FALSE 2500 100000 FALSE EQUALS `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 89(1), (3)(a), r 9)`
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` FALSE FALSE 2500 100000 FALSE EQUALS `no: only a party, or a person in whose favour the order was made, is recognised to recover it (s 89(1), r 9)`
-- r 9: a person in whose favour the order was made need not be a party.
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` FALSE TRUE 2500 100000 FALSE EQUALS `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 89(1), (3)(a), r 9)`
-- s 38I(1): a purported monetary order, recoverable only by the person in whose favour it was made, in the s 89 court.
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` TRUE TRUE 100000 100000 TRUE EQUALS `you can recover it as a debt in the Magistrates Court (s 38I(1), s 89(3)(a))`
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` FALSE TRUE 100001 100000 TRUE EQUALS `you can recover it as a debt in the District Court (s 38I(1), s 89(3)(b))`
#ASSERT `Where can I recover the money` TRUE FALSE 2500 100000 TRUE EQUALS `no: only the person in whose favour a purported monetary order was made can recover it (s 38I(1))`

§§ `ss 38I(2), 40(4), 41(3), 60(3), 89(2), 93A, 93B: offences`

`oath` MEANS `refused or failed to take an oath or affirmation when required (s 40(4)(a))`
`produce` MEANS `failed to produce evidentiary material I was required to produce (s 40(4)(b))`
`summons` MEANS `failed to appear in answer to a summons (s 40(4)(c))`
`answer` MEANS `failed to give evidence or answer a question put in the proceedings (s 40(4)(d))`
`false evidence` MEANS `gave false or misleading evidence to the Tribunal (s 40(4)(e))`
`obstruct` MEANS `obstructed a member or authorised person entering or inspecting property (s 41(3))`
`privacy` MEANS `did not comply with a direction for a private hearing or restricting publication or disclosure (s 60(3))`
`order` MEANS `did not comply with an order of the Tribunal, other than an order to pay money (s 89(2))`
`purported` MEANS `did not comply with a purported order of the Tribunal (one invalid for want of federal jurisdiction), other than a purported monetary order (s 38I(2))`
`disrupt` MEANS `wilfully interrupted proceedings, or used offensive language or behaved in a disorderly or offensive manner (s 93A)`
`fee lie` MEANS `knowingly made a false or misleading statement to have fees waived, remitted or deferred (s 93B)`
`excused` MEANS `no: it is an offence only if done without reasonable excuse, and you had one (s 40(4)(b)-(d))`

#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `produce` TRUE EQUALS `excused`
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `summons` TRUE EQUALS `excused`
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `answer` TRUE EQUALS `excused`
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `summons` FALSE EQUALS `yes: that is an offence`
-- (a) and (e) carry no reasonable-excuse words.
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `oath` TRUE EQUALS `yes: that is an offence`
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `false evidence` TRUE EQUALS `yes: that is an offence`
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `order` TRUE EQUALS `yes: that is an offence`
#ASSERT `Is what I did an offence` `purported` TRUE EQUALS `yes: that is an offence`

#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `oath` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `produce` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `summons` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `answer` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `false evidence` EQUALS 25000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `obstruct` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `privacy` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `order` EQUALS 50000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `purported` EQUALS 50000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `disrupt` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT `What is the largest fine` `fee lie` EQUALS 1250

#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `oath` EQUALS 12
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `false evidence` EQUALS 12
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `obstruct` EQUALS 6
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `privacy` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `order` EQUALS 24
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `purported` EQUALS 24
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `disrupt` EQUALS 6
#ASSERT `What is the longest prison term in months` `fee lie` EQUALS 3

§§ `r 14: fees`

`app fee` MEANS `the fee to start proceedings`
`referral fee` MEANS `the fee for referring a residential park, tenancy or rooming house matter`
`owner` MEANS `the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor`
`third party` MEANS `the third party named in the referral subsection`
`tenant` MEANS `someone else, such as the resident or tenant`
`not me 14(4)` MEANS `no: the referral fee is paid by the park owner, landlord or rooming house proprietor, or in some cases the third party, not by you (r 14(4))`
`ir fee` MEANS `the fee for an internal review under s 70`
`transcript` MEANS `the fee for a transcript`
`payable` MEANS `yes: the fee is payable (the Registrar may waive, remit or postpone it for financial hardship or in the interests of justice, r 14(6))`

#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `app fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `app fee` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: you are a body exempt from the Tribunal's fees (r 14(1))`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `app fee` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: fees are not payable in proceedings under the Advance Care Directives, Consent to Medical Treatment and Palliative Care, Guardianship and Administration or Mental Health Acts (r 14(2))`
-- r 14(2) carves out the internal review and transcript fees.
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `transcript` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `ir fee` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `ir fee` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: as the person the proceedings are about, you need not pay the internal review fee (r 14(3))`
-- r 14(3) only in those four Acts.
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `ir fee` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `app fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Registrar or a member has waived or remitted it`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `app fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `deferred: payment has been postponed`
-- r 14(4)(b), (d), (f): a referral under any other provision of RPA s 29, RTA s 63 or s 105M: the owner, landlord or
-- proprietor pays, not the tenant or resident.
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `tenant` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not me 14(4)`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `owner` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `third party` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not me 14(4)`
-- r 14(4)(a), (c), (e): under RPA s 29(6a), RTA s 63(7) or s 105M(8), the owner if it lodged a notice of dispute,
-- otherwise the third party.
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `owner` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `third party` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not me 14(4)`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `third party` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `payable`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `owner` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not me 14(4)`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `tenant` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not me 14(4)`
-- "Subject to this regulation": an exempt owner (the South Australian Housing Trust, r 14(1)(f)) pays nothing; a liable
-- owner can still have it waived; the r 14(4) payer test does not touch the other fees.
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `owner` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: you are a body exempt from the Tribunal's fees (r 14(1))`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `owner` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Registrar or a member has waived or remitted it`
#ASSERT `Must I pay the fee` `ir fee` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `owner` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `payable`

#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `app fee` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: your proceedings are stayed until the fee is paid, waived or postponed (r 14(5))`
#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `referral fee` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: your proceedings are stayed until the fee is paid, waived or postponed (r 14(5))`
#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `ir fee` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: your proceedings are stayed until the fee is paid, waived or postponed (r 14(5))`
#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `app fee` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the fee has been paid, waived or postponed`
#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `app fee` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a Presidential member has determined otherwise (r 14(5))`
#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `transcript` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not under r 14(5): the stay applies only to the application, referral, internal review and listed review fees`
#ASSERT `Is my case on hold until I pay` `another prescribed fee` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not under r 14(5): the stay applies only to the application, referral, internal review and listed review fees`
